---
import Chapter from '../Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../Figure.astro';
import FigFederationModels from '../figures/FigFederationModels.astro';
import FigTwoPaths from '../figures/FigTwoPaths.astro';

// The closest related work, and what each one leaves open for this problem.
const related = [
  {
    who: 'RFC 5280, section 6',
    does: 'Validates a certification path in full detail.',
    open: 'Its first input is the path, already built. It neither searches nor prefers.',
    ref: 'RFC5280',
  },
  {
    who: 'RFC 4158',
    does: 'Describes how to build paths and how to sort candidates heuristically.',
    open: 'Informational. It gives no criterion that makes one valid path better than another.',
    ref: 'RFC4158',
  },
  {
    who: 'SCVP, RFC 5055',
    does: 'Lets a client delegate path construction and ask a server for the best path.',
    open: 'Never defines what best means, and needs a certificate that already exists.',
    ref: 'RFC5055',
  },
  {
    who: 'ARMOR and Verdict',
    does: 'Formally verified validators: the acceptance decision is proved against a specification.',
    open: 'They verify whether a chain is acceptable. They do not order the acceptable ones.',
    ref: 'ARMOR2024',
    ref2: 'Verdict2025',
  },
];
---

<Chapter id="landscape" num="2" kicker="Where federation stands" title="Trust already crosses domains, along paths nobody chose">
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      A PKI binds a public key to an identity, and trust in that binding climbs a chain of
      certificates up to an anchor that the verifier has chosen. Inside one organisation the problem is solved; the difficulty begins when <strong>sovereign domains, each with its own policies and its own
      cryptography, need to accept each other’s identities</strong>.
      <span class="sn"
        ><b>Where the crossings are.</b> Cross-certificates are issued between intermediate CAs, never from a root: the root stays offline and only certifies its own subordinates.</span
      >
    </p>
    <p>
      Four models do this today, as figure 1 shows: two of them link the domains with certificates, either through a mesh of bilateral cross-certificates or through a bridge CA in the middle, while the other two rely on a list or an agreement and bound nothing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure num={1} class="figure--text" caption="The four federation models, after the World Bank taxonomy <a href='#ref-WorldBank'>[WorldBank]</a>.">
    <FigFederationModels />
  </Figure>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Once several domains cross-certify, the hierarchy becomes a directed graph, and one credential
      can have more than one valid path. The Federal PKI playbook shows the same certificate
      validating through three CAs or through six, with two bridges on the longer route, and which of the two applies <strong>depends only on the anchor that is installed</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure num={2} caption="One credential, two valid paths. Redrawn from the Federal PKI path discovery and validation playbook <a href='#ref-FPKI-PDVAL'>[FPKI-PDVAL]</a>.">
    <FigTwoPaths />
  </Figure>

  <p class="key">If there are several paths, someone has to choose among them, and today nobody has written down how.</p>

  <h3 class="sub">Related work</h3>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The standards and the verified validators answer a different question from the one asked
      here. A validator, verified or not, takes a chain and a policy and says yes or no. This work
      takes a federation and a receiver and says which chains exist for a certificate, which one the
      receiver prefers, and what would change if a cross-certificate were issued. The two are
      complementary: the path the model selects still has to pass a validator, and a verified one
      is a candidate for that final check.
      <span class="sn"
        ><b>Verdict</b> is the closest work to this one, although the question it answers is a different one. Its policy is a first-order predicate over one chain and its path builder is specified over the paths that repeat no certificate, so the universe searched here (paths that repeat no key) is a subset of Verdict’s.</span
      >
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="tbl">
      <caption><b>Table 1.</b> What the closest work does, and what it leaves open.</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Work</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">What it leaves open</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          related.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                {r.who} <a href={`#ref-${r.ref}`}>[{r.ref}]</a>
                {r.ref2 && <a href={`#ref-${r.ref2}`}>[{r.ref2}]</a>}
              </th>
              <td>{r.does}</td>
              <td>{r.open}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</Chapter>
